# Code owners for agentic-foundry.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT DO — stated plainly, because a CODEOWNERS file that people believe
# is enforcing when it is not is worse than no file at all.
#
# GitHub only *requires* code-owner review when the branch protection rule for `main` sets
# `require_code_owner_review: true`. It is currently FALSE here: this repository runs a
# solo-operator model, and requiring a code-owner review would mean requiring a second person who
# does not exist — the merge would be unsatisfiable rather than protected.
#
# So today this file does two real things and one theoretical one:
#   * it requests the owner as a reviewer automatically on any PR touching these paths, which is
#     the reminder that matters when the fleet is running many parallel sessions;
#   * it is the machine-readable record of which paths are considered privileged surface;
#   * and it becomes ENFORCING the moment a second maintainer exists and
#     `require_code_owner_review` is turned on — no further edit needed.
#
# Recorded as a Tier-A precondition at the 2026-07-27 floor-#3 review ("CODEOWNERS on .github/").

# Default owner for everything not matched more specifically below.
*                       @lukasrepublic

# CI and automation — the merge floor itself. A change here can weaken every other gate, and one
# of these workflows now holds `id-token: write` and publishes to a public registry.
/.github/               @lukasrepublic

# The plugin manifest and the shipped hook wiring — prompt-level and load-time executable surface.
/.claude-plugin/        @lukasrepublic
/hooks/                 @lukasrepublic

# Authorization, contracts and the merge gate — the front-authorization floor's own machinery.
/scripts/foundry_authz.py       @lukasrepublic
/scripts/foundry-authorize.py   @lukasrepublic
/scripts/foundry_contract.py    @lukasrepublic

# The publishable CLI: what an adopter executes on a clean machine, before any session exists.
/cli/                   @lukasrepublic
